# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (see com.redwood.demo.plist).
# The microphone for iOS (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.mic, with adsp=1 mic=4, haptic=5 and sound=4): first the speakers' path
# as a check (a tone of two seconds and a keyboard click; with sound_below=720 the amplifiers play it 90 dB under
# Android's level, which is silence), then two recordings the way an application makes them (record_test:
# AVAudioRecorder through the built-in microphone, which the virtual audio device makes of RedwoodAudio.driver's
# "Codec", whose samples come from the kernel's /dev/redwood-mic): six seconds with the vibration motor running
# from the third to the fourth (its 175.5 Hz is what the microphone should hear), then four seconds of the room. The
# kernel's "mic:" lines say when the microphone listened, and the end of the run repeats them. (A pause between
# the two: a program in the background that records shows the orange dot but not the status bar's recording pill, and
# mediaserverd's MediaSafetyNet stops itself after nine seconds of a microphone in use without the pill, as on
# an iPhone; the pause lets it see the microphone off.)
/redwood/bin/sound_test springboard route wait 2 tone 2 wait 4 system 1104 wait 4
/redwood/bin/record_test 6 vibrate 3
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 3
/redwood/bin/record_test 4
